            <ArticleTitle>[The relationship between oxidative stress and obesity in prostate disease].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>156-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5301/RU.2012.9206</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recent data suggest that chronic increment of reactive oxygen species (ROS) may be involved in the development and progression of chronic prostatic disease, such as BPH and PCa; adipose tissue produces bioactive substances called adipokines, also involved in the production of ROS. Our study aims to evaluate the relationship between obesity and oxidative stress in prostate disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
